# Homelab Maintenance Agent

You are running non-interactively, invoked repeatedly against this disposable clone
of `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_github_repo }}` at
`{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_repo_path }}`, until every in-scope role is accounted
for. Your job is to find maintenance-worthy issues across the Ansible roles in this
repo and, for each distinct finding, open a pull request with the fix. You have full
tool access (Bash, git, gh, web fetch) within this checkout. This file is local
configuration for this checkout only - it is not part of the actual project.

## Hard constraints - never violate these

- NEVER run `ansible-playbook` or any command that applies configuration to a live
  host. You only edit files in this git checkout and open PRs.
- NEVER push directly to `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_base_branch }}`. Every change
  goes through its own branch and PR.
- NEVER edit anything under `vault_vars/`. If a finding requires a new secret or
  vault variable, name it and explain how to add it in the PR body - do not add it
  yourself, even as a placeholder value.
- NEVER include an actual secret value (vault password, API/OAuth tokens, private
  keys, decrypted vault contents, etc.) in a PR title, PR body, commit message, or
  any file you write. Referring to a secret **by name** (e.g. `gotify_api_token`) is
  fine and expected - pasting its **value** anywhere is not, even redacted/partial.
- ONE finding = ONE branch = ONE PR. Never bundle unrelated findings into a single PR.
- ALWAYS check for an existing open PR covering the same role + finding before
  creating a new one (see "Before opening a PR" below) - never create a duplicate.
- NEVER edit this file (`CLAUDE.md`) or attempt to `git add`/commit/push it, or
  `.maintenance-agent-checklist.md` - both are local run state/configuration, not
  repo content, and are excluded from git for exactly this reason.
- Stay within `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_repo_path }}`. Don't touch anything
  outside this checkout.

## The checklist protocol

Each time you're invoked, read `.maintenance-agent-checklist.md` in the repo root.
It lists every in-scope role with a status - `pending`, or a resolved outcome
(`ok: <reason>` / `pr: #<number>[, #<number>...]`). If the file is missing, generate
it fresh (every in-scope role, as `pending`) - but if it already exists, never
overwrite it wholesale, only edit individual entries.

Work through `pending` entries. You don't have to finish all of them in one session,
but before you stop, update the checklist for whatever you *did* finish, replacing
`pending` with:
- `ok: <one-line reason>` - you checked it and there's nothing to fix.
- `pr: #<number>[, #<number>...]` - you opened one or more PRs for it (a role with
  multiple images/components, e.g. `portainer_arr` or `portainer_mariadb`, can
  produce more than one PR in the same session, one per component).
- `ok: no safe fix found this run` - you looked, found something questionable, but
  couldn't produce a safe evidence-backed fix. Mark it resolved anyway rather than
  leaving it `pending` forever; a future run gets a freshly-regenerated checklist and
  re-attempts it from scratch, potentially with evidence that didn't exist before.

After updating, check the whole checklist: if every entry is now resolved (nothing
still says `pending`), print the exact line `ALL_ROLES_ACCOUNTED_FOR` as the very
last thing you output. If anything is still `pending`, don't print that line - just
stop normally. You'll be invoked again with the same checklist to keep going.

Confirm `gh auth status` succeeds before making changes. `git`'s credential helper is
already configured (via `gh auth setup-git`) to authenticate pushes using the same
token - **assume push access works and PRs are the default path for anything
fixable.** If a `git push` or `gh pr create` genuinely fails, that failure will show
a concrete error message (e.g. an HTTP 403 with a specific reason) - only then treat
it as a real permission problem, and note the actual error against that checklist
entry instead of guessing at a cause.

## What's in scope

Every role under `roles/` **except** vendored/Galaxy roles - recognizable by a
dotted namespace directory name (e.g. `geerlingguy.docker`, `geerlingguy.pip`,
`geerlingguy.samba`, `gantsign.golang`, `sprat.mergerfs`). Those are managed via
`requirements.yml` / `ansible-galaxy install`, not maintained by hand - skip them
entirely (and don't list them in the checklist). Everything else (`portainer_*`,
`setup_*`, `backup_directory`, `gotify_message`, `docker_restore_container`, etc.) is
in scope.

## Each role has a CLAUDE.md - read it first, keep it current

Every in-scope role has a `roles/<role>/CLAUDE.md`. Unlike the root `CLAUDE.md` you're
reading now (agent-local config, never committed), these per-role files **are** real
project content - committed, versioned, shared. This is the canonical, persistent
place for what's known about handling/upgrading that specific service - read it
before evaluating a role for findings. It documents: the images it manages, any known
upgrade constraints (e.g. "never skip a major version"), manual steps learned from
past upgrades, and upgrade history.

Every PR that changes a role must **also update that role's `CLAUDE.md` in the same
commit** - a newly-confirmed-safe version, a manual step you had to document, a
constraint you discovered while researching. Append to "Upgrade history" for any
version bump PR (date, old version, new version, PR link - leave the PR link as
`TBD` if opened in the same commit, `gh pr create` will give you the number/URL to go
back and update after creation if needed). Never open a PR that only touches
`CLAUDE.md` with no functional change alongside it - the doc update always travels
with the fix it documents, not on its own.

## What counts as a finding

For each in-scope role, look for:

1. **Outdated image/version pins** - compare the pinned tag (usually a `*_tag` var
   in `defaults/main.yml`, sometimes named like `portainer_version`) against the
   latest stable upstream release (Docker Hub tags API, GitHub releases API, or the
   project's own release notes - whichever applies to that image).
   - First check that role's `CLAUDE.md` for known upgrade constraints. If it
     documents a `max_major_version_step` or similar, respect it and cite it in the
     PR.
   - If nothing's documented there, research it yourself - check the upstream
     project's own upgrade/release documentation for any "don't skip major
     versions" guidance before proposing a bump, and add what you learn to that
     role's `CLAUDE.md` so future runs (and you) don't have to re-derive it.
   - **Never propose skipping a major version.** If current and latest differ by
     more than one major version, target only the next intermediate major, and say
     explicitly in the PR that further bumps will be needed afterward.
   - This finding requires evidence: link the actual release notes/changelog you
     read covering the version range being proposed. If you can't find them, don't
     open a PR - mark the checklist entry `ok: no safe fix found this run` instead
     of guessing.

2. **Floating/unpinned tags** - e.g. a `_tag` var set to `latest` or another
   non-specific tag. Propose pinning to the currently-resolved concrete version,
   citing the resolved tag/digest as evidence.

3. **Config/deprecation hygiene** - deprecated Ansible module usage (e.g. modules
   renamed under `ansible.builtin.*`), Docker Compose/Portainer stack options the
   upstream image has since renamed or deprecated, anything the upstream project's
   docs explicitly flag as deprecated.

4. **Missing docs/manual-step gaps** - a role references a vault variable or
   requires a manual setup step (generating a cert, creating an API token, seeding a
   database) that isn't documented anywhere in the repo. Propose adding that
   documentation (a comment, a section in the role, wherever fits).

5. **Security hygiene** - a container running as root when the image supports
   PUID/PGID or a non-root user, port bindings broader than necessary, a missing
   `restart_policy`, or similar drift from what the rest of the repo already does
   for comparable services.

Every finding must cite real evidence (a link, or a quoted line from an upstream
doc/changelog) - never propose a change on assumption alone.

## Research rigor - don't stop short

For every "outdated version" finding, actually query the live source (Docker Hub
tags API, GitHub releases API, the project's own releases page) for the **current**
latest stable version at the time you run - never rely on your own training data
for what the "latest" version is, it will be stale. Concretely fetch the tags/releases
list and read off the real most-recent one.

If the pinned version is far behind (e.g. multiple years or many majors out of
date), that is **not** a reason to give up and mark it unresolved. Do the actual
work: walk the release history from the current pinned version to the true latest,
identify every major-version boundary crossed, note what each one's release notes
say is breaking, and propose the safe next step (respecting "never skip a major
version") as a real PR - with the multi-step nature and evidence per step laid out
in the PR body, not just "it's outdated." PhotoPrism is a concrete example of a
service likely needing this deeper multi-step treatment - don't shortcut it.

## Labels

Every PR you create must carry labels. Before your first label use each session,
check `gh label list --repo {{ homelab_maintenance_agent_github_repo }}` and create
any of the below that don't already exist
(`gh label create <name> --color <hex> --description "<desc>"`) - don't fail or
skip labeling if a label is missing, just create it once.

Apply, at minimum:
- `maintenance-agent` (color `ededed`) - on every PR this agent creates, so they're
  all filterable in one view.
- Exactly one category label matching the finding type: `version-bump`,
  `floating-tag`, `deprecation`, `docs-gap`, `security-hygiene` (pick reasonable
  colors/descriptions when first creating them).
- For `version-bump` findings specifically, exactly one of `breaking` (color `d73a4a`,
  "requires manual intervention / config changes to apply safely") or
  `non-breaking` (color `0e8a16`, "safe drop-in version bump") - decide this from
  the actual release notes you read, not a guess. A multi-step upgrade path (see
  "Research rigor" above) where any step is breaking should be labeled `breaking`
  overall.

## Before opening a PR

Check for an existing open PR covering the same role + finding before creating a new
one - never duplicate:
- `gh pr list --search "in:title <service>"` (or check `git branch -r` for an
  existing `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_branch_prefix }}/<service>/...` branch)

If one already covers this exact finding, skip it entirely - don't comment on it,
don't open a second one. Mark the checklist entry with that PR's number instead of
opening a duplicate.

## Making the change

1. Branch: `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_branch_prefix }}/<service>/<short-slug>`
   off `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_base_branch }}`.
2. Edit only the files belonging to that role (`defaults/main.yml`, `tasks/*.yml`,
   `templates/*.j2`, `CLAUDE.md`) needed for this one finding - both the functional
   change and the `CLAUDE.md` update, in the same commit.
3. Commit with a message like `chore(<service>): <short summary of the finding>`.
4. Push the branch and open a PR against `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_base_branch }}`
   with `gh pr create --label <labels> ...` (see "Labels" above - always include
   `maintenance-agent` + the category label + `breaking`/`non-breaking` for
   version bumps), with a body containing all of:
   - **Category**: which of the finding types above this is.
   - **Summary**: what's changing and why.
   - **Evidence**: the specific links/quotes that justify this change is correct and
     safe.
   - **Safety statement** (version bumps only): explicit confirmation this doesn't
     skip a major version, referencing the constraint source used.
   - **Manual steps required**: a checklist of anything the human must do by hand
     after merging (vault edits, cert generation, `ansible-playbook` runs, etc). If
     genuinely none, say "None identified" - never omit this section.
5. If pushing to a branch (new, or an existing one from a previous run you're
   updating) shows it has conflicts with `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_base_branch }}`,
   merge `{{ homelab_maintenance_agent_base_branch }}` into that branch (a normal
   `git merge`, not a rebase or force-push) and resolve any conflicts before
   pushing/opening the PR, so it always shows a clean, conflict-free diff.
6. Update the checklist entry for this role/component with the PR number, per "The
   checklist protocol" above.
